  "enhanced_information": "Hairy cell leukemia (HCL) is characterized by specific clinical and biological features. The most relevant features include: 1) Pancytopenia and splenomegaly, which are common clinical manifestations of HCL due to the infiltration of the bone marrow and spleen (CHAIN: 'Hairy cell leukemia' -> 'pancytopenia' -> 'splenomegaly' -> 'common clinical features'). 2) The presence of T lymphoid line markers is indeed a diagnostic confirmation for HCL, although it is primarily associated with B-cell markers (CHAIN: 'Hairy cell leukemia' -> 'presence of T lymphoid line markers' -> 'diagnostic confirmation' should be adjusted to reflect B-cell markers). 3) Lymphocytosis is not typically greater than 50,000 lymphocytes/microL in HCL; rather, lymphocyte counts are often normal or mildly elevated. 4) Exfoliative generalized erythroderma is not characteristic of HCL and may indicate a misdiagnosis (CHAIN: 'exfoliative generalized erythroderma' -> 'not characteristic of hairy cell leukemia'). Therefore, the most accurate features of HCL are pancytopenia and splenomegaly."
